          "question": "Which conditions covered here have a three-character code that takes no further character?",
          "answer": "From the retrieved coding guidance, the one condition explicitly described as a three-character code with nothing further is essential (primary) hypertension, coded I10. The document \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\" says I10 \"takes none\" of a fourth, fifth or sixth character, so it is complete at three characters.\n\nThe other documents I retrieved do not list which conditions have a three-character code with no further characters. The structure guide (\"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\") says three characters is the category level and that many codes add more detail. The type 2 diabetes guidance (\"Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories\") shows that E11 codes often need extra characters. The endocrine chapter overview (\"ICD-10-CM Chapter 4\") lists category ranges but does not say which ones stand alone. So this answer may not be complete.\n\nThis is general coding information, not a diagnosis. If you have questions about a specific condition or code, please raise them with a clinician or a qualified medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
